// fern-coreutils.fern — every utility in `coreutils/` in one binary.
//
// The binary decides which utility to be from the basename of argv[0],
// so it is used through symlinks named after the utilities:
//
//     $ ln -s fern-coreutils yes
//     $ ./yes | head -2
//     y
//     y
//
// That is the whole dispatch, and it is why nothing here has to thread
// a program name anywhere: `gnu.prog()` reads argv[0], which under a
// symlink is already the utility's own name, path included. A usage
// error from the multicall binary is byte-for-byte the one the
// standalone build prints.
//
// Run under its own name the binary is not a utility, and answers for
// itself: `--list` prints the catalogue one per line, `--help` and
// `--version` do the usual. Running a utility by naming it as an
// argument (`fern-coreutils yes`) is NOT supported — a utility invoked
// that way would see this binary's name in its diagnostics, and there
// is no `set_program_name` to reach for because Fern has no
// module-level mutable state; #9694 is the runtime primitive that would
// lift it. The release artifact ships the symlinks beside the binary,
// so the supported path is the only one needed meanwhile.
//
// The imports below are the catalogue, and
// `internal/coreutils/multicall_test.go` fails when a `coreutils/*.fern`
// is missing from it — the list is checked in rather than generated so
// that it is reviewable, and gated so that it cannot drift.
//
// Aliased imports throughout: a module name is bound as an identifier,
// and `[`, `true` and `false` are not identifiers.
